    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3253",
      "problem": "In a right triangle $\\triangle ABC,$ we have $AC = 9,$ $BC=13,$ and $m\\angle A = \\alpha.$ Given that $\\overline{AB}$ is the hypotenuse of the triangle, find the value of $\\alpha$ (rounded to one decimal place).",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the triangle that's described.\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14209655/assets/29ab71a468b9a57842e07ef74b8d8d770c7ebfacabb3735694c0dd40303ad220.png)\n\nSince we are given the lengths of the adjacent and opposite sides, we can use tangent to find the angle measurement. We have\n\n$$\n\\tan \\alpha=  \\dfrac{\\textrm{opposite}}{\\textrm{adjacent}}  = \\dfrac{13}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe now calculate $\\alpha$ using the inverse tangent:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\tan \\alpha &= \\dfrac{13}{9}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\arctan( \\tan \\alpha) &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{13}{9} \\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\alpha &= \\arctan\\left(\\dfrac{13}{9} \\right)\\[5pt]\n&=(55.304\\,8...)^\\circ\\[5pt]\t\n\t\t &\\approx 55.3^\\circ\n\t}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
